        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we&#x27;ve done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we&#x27;ve learned for quadratic expressions.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, consider the polynomial <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x^3-2x.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "For example, consider the polynomial [MATH: 2x^3-2x.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x.]</span> To simplify the computation, we can factor out the <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x,]</span> as follows:</p>",
            "readable_text": "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of [MATH: 2x.] To simplify the computation, we can factor out the [MATH: 2x,] as follows:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Now, we factor the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x^2-1]</span> using the difference of squares formula:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Now, we factor the expression [MATH: x^2-1] using the difference of squares formula:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>So, the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x^3-2x]</span> factors into <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2x(x+1)(x-1).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "So, the expression [MATH: 2x^3-2x] factors into [MATH: 2x(x+1)(x-1).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nContent:\nFor example, consider the polynomial [MATH: 2x^3-2x.]\n\nContent:\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of [MATH: 2x.] To simplify the computation, we can factor out the [MATH: 2x,] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)]\n\nContent:\nNow, we factor the expression [MATH: x^2-1] using the difference of squares formula:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)]\n\nContent:\nSo, the expression [MATH: 2x^3-2x] factors into [MATH: 2x(x+1)(x-1).]"
      },
