    ],
    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nContent:\nFor example, consider the polynomial [MATH: 2x^3-2x.]\n\nContent:\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of [MATH: 2x.] To simplify the computation, we can factor out the [MATH: 2x,] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)]\n\nContent:\nNow, we factor the expression [MATH: x^2-1] using the difference of squares formula:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)]\n\nContent:\nSo, the expression [MATH: 2x^3-2x] factors into [MATH: 2x(x+1)(x-1).]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Factoring Using Common Factors and Differences of Squares\n\nExample Prompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: x^3 - 4x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of [MATH: x.] To simplify the computation, we can factor out the [MATH: x,] as follows:\n\n[MATH: x^3 - 4x =\nx(x^2 - 4)]\nNow, we factor the expression [MATH: x^2 - 4] using the difference of squares formula:\n\n[MATH: x(x^2 - 4) = x(x+2)(x-2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: 4x^{3} \u2212 x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{2}(2x + 1)]\nb. [MATH: x(2x + 1)(2x \u2212 1)]\nc. [MATH: x^{2}(2x + 1)(2x \u2212 1)]\nd. [MATH: x(2x \u2212 1)^{2}]\ne. [MATH: x^{2}(2x \u2212 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: x^{5} \u2212 x^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{2}(x + 1)^{3}]\nb. [MATH: x^{3}(x \u2212 1)^{2}]\nc. [MATH: x^{3}(x \u2212 1)]\nd. [MATH: x^{2}(x + 1)(x \u2212 1)]\ne. [MATH: x^{3}(x + 1)(x \u2212 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Factoring Using Common Factors and Perfect Square Trinomials\n\nExample Prompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of [MATH: x.] To simplify the computation, we can factor out the [MATH: x,] as follows:\n\n[MATH: x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)]\n\nThe expression [MATH: x^2 - 6x + 9] is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n[MATH: x(x^{2} \u2212 6x + 9)|=; x(x^{2} \u2212 6x + 3^{2})|=; x(x^{2} \u2212 2 \u22c5 3x + 3^{2})|=; x(x \u2212 3)^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: 9x^{3} \u2212 12x^{2} + 4x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x(3x + 2)^{2}]\nb. [MATH: x^{2}(3x + 2)]\nc. [MATH: x^{2}(3x \u2212 2)^{2}]\nd. [MATH: x^{2}(3x \u2212 2)]\ne. [MATH: x(3x \u2212 2)^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: x^{4} + 2x^{3} + x^{2}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x(x + 1)^{3}]\nb. [MATH: x^{3}(x \u2212 1)]\nc. [MATH: x^{2}(x \u2212 1)^{2}]\nd. [MATH: x^{3}(x + 1)]\ne. [MATH: x^{2}(x + 1)^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Factoring Using Common Factors and Quadratic Factoring\n\nExample Prompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: x^3 + x^2 - 2x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nNote that all of the terms have a common factor of [MATH: x.] To simplify the computation, we can factor out the [MATH: x,] as follows:\n\n[MATH: x^{3} + x^{2} \u2212 2x|= x(x^{2} + x \u2212 2)]\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial [MATH: x^2 + x - 2] as follows:\n[MATH: x(x^{2} + x \u2212 2)|=; x((x^{2} \u2212 x) + (2x \u2212 2))|=; x(x(x \u2212 1) + 2(x \u2212 1))|=; x(x + 2)(x \u2212 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: x^{5} + 4x^{4} + 3x^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{2}(x + 3)(x^{2} + 1)]\nb. [MATH: x^{3}(x + 3)^{2}]\nc. [MATH: x^{3}(x + 3)(x + 1)]\nd. [MATH: x^{2}(x^{2} + 3)(x + 1)]\ne. [MATH: x(x^{2} + 3)(x^{2} + 1)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nFactor the expression [MATH: 3x^{3} + 2x^{2} \u2212 x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x(3x \u2212 1)^{2}]\nb. [MATH: x(3x + 1)(x \u2212 1)]\nc. [MATH: (3x \u2212 1)^{2}(x + 1)]\nd. [MATH: (3x \u2212 1)(x + 1)^{2}]\ne. [MATH: x(3x \u2212 1)(x + 1)]"
  }
